Frontend Testing:  Task Manager App
1. Introduction
This document shows how we tested the frontend of the Task Manager App. Testing makes sure the buttons, forms, and pages work correctly for the user.
 Unit Tests:  Test small parts, like a single button or a date format.
 Integration Tests: Test how parts work together, like typing a task and seeing it on the list.
 2. Test Cases
2.1. Adding Tasks
TC-01: Empty Form
 What we tested: Submitting a blank task.
 Steps: We clicked the “Add Task” button without typing anything in the input box.
  Result:  The app blocks the submission and shows an error message like “Title is required”.

TC-02: Add Valid Task
 What we tested: Adding a normal task successfully.
 Steps: We typed a task title into the input box and click the “Add Task” button.
 Result: The new task appears on the list. The input box becomes empty.

 2.2. Managing Tasks
TC-03: Complete Task
 What we tested: Marking a task as done.
 Steps: We clicked the checkbox or complete button on a task.
 Result: The task changes color or gets a line through the text to show it is finished.

TC-04: Edit Task
 What we tested: Changing the text of a task.
 Steps: We clicked the “Edit” button, type new text, and click “Save”.
 Result: The task shows the new text on the screen immediately.

TC-05: Delete Task
 What we tested: Removing a task.
 Steps: We clicked the “Delete” button on a task.
  Result: The task disappears from the screen completely.

2.3. Search and Filters
TC-06: Search Bar
 What we tested: Finding a task by typing.
 Steps: We typed a specific word into the search box.
 Result: The screen updates to show only the tasks that match that word.

TC-07: Filter Tabs
 What we tested: Filtering by status.
 Steps: We clicked on the “Completed” tab.
  Result: The screen hides active tasks and only shows finished tasks.

TC-08: No Results
 What we tested: Searching for a word that does not exist.
 Steps: We typed a random word into the search box.
 Result: The list becomes empty and shows a text message saying “No tasks found”.

 3. Code Example
This is a simple code example to test adding a task:

```javascript
Import { render, screen } from ‘@testing-library/react’;
Import userEvent from ‘@testing-library/user-event’;
Import App from ‘./App’;

Test(‘User can add a new task’, async () => {
  Render(<App />);

  // 1. Find the input box and the button
  Const input = screen.getByPlaceholderText(‘Type a task...’);
  Const button = screen.getByText(‘Add Task’);

  // 2. Simulate the user typing and clicking
  Await userEvent.type(input, ‘Buy milk’);
  Await userEvent.click(button);

  // 3. Check if the task is on the screen
  Expect(screen.getByText(‘Buy milk’)).toBeInTheDocument();
});

```


